<chapter xml:id="ch01-velocity-distance-and-the-first-shape-of-calculus" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Velocity, Distance, and the First Shape of Calculus</title>

  <introduction>
    <!-- CONVERTED — VISUALLY VERIFIED -->
    <p>A car dashboard already contains the first story of calculus. One instrument remembers how much road has been accumulated. Another tells how fast that accumulated amount is changing.</p>

    <p>Those two ideas are not separate. They are inverse clues:</p>

    <md>
      <mrow>\text{distance changes} \amp\quad \longleftrightarrow \quad \text{velocity is measured},</mrow>
      <mrow>\text{velocity is accumulated} \amp\quad \longleftrightarrow \quad \text{distance changes}.</mrow>
    </md>

    <p>Calculus begins by making this relation precise.</p>
  </introduction>

